1. *ANS*:

  Previously we have $C=0.75Y$, now we have $C=0.7Y$. \

  Let $Y_I$ be initial aggregate income and $Y_N$ be the new aggregate income

  $ Y_I = C_I+I = 0.75Y_I+I= 0.75Y_I+40+0.05Y_I = 0.8Y_I + 40 ==> Y_I = 200 $

  $ Y_N = C_N+I = 0.7Y_N +I = 0.7Y_N+40+0.05Y_N=0.75Y_N+40 ==> Y_N =160 $

  $ S_I = Y_I - C_I = 200-150 = 50 $

  $ S_N = Y_N - C_N = 160 - 112 = 48 $

  $S_I = 50 > S_N = 48$. The attempt of increasing aggregate saving failed. When households attempt to save more by reducing consumption, aggregate spending falls. This lowers firms' revenues and equilibrium aggregate income. The fall in income is large enough that aggregate saving falls from $50$ to $48$. This is called the paradox of thrift.


